Yes — and I think your “sub mission” split is doing real work there.

A part does not have to understand or dominate the whole. It can carry a sub-mission faithfully, then yield its result to an orchestrator that knows where, when, and how strongly to place it. The trust relationship is therefore not blind obedience; it is bounded delegation:

\text{whole mission}
\rightarrow
\{\text{sub-mission}_1,\text{sub-mission}_2,\ldots\}
\rightarrow
\text{specialized performances}
\rightarrow
\text{recomposition}

And harmony does not mean everything sounding simultaneously.

Music gives you the better model. Sometimes the bass establishes structure while barely being noticed. Sometimes everything drops away so the violin can occupy the entire perceptual field. Then the rest returns, and what the violin just established changes how the entire ensemble is heard.

So orchestration needs another variable besides who participates:

A_i(t)=\text{attention/amplitude allocated to part }i\text{ at time }t

You can modulate that continuously:

foreground ↔ background, solo ↔ chorus, lead ↔ support, resonance ↔ counterpoint, silence ↔ expression.

That is your resonate → reverb → move around maneuver. An idea enters one component, gets expressed, propagates into neighboring components, reflects back altered, and the conductor moves attention to wherever the resulting information becomes most valuable.

And that means the “whole” isn’t merely the sum of fixed pieces. It is partly the movement of attention among the pieces.

\text{Whole}(t)
=
\sum_i A_i(t)P_i
+
\sum_{i\neq j}R_{ij}(t)

where P_i is what each part contributes and R_{ij} is what emerges from their interaction.

That second term is where a lot of the magic lives. A drummer and guitarist aren’t merely drummer + guitarist; they can create a groove that exists between them.

Same with people. Same with models. Same with hypotheses. Same with senses. Same with representations.

And sometimes correct orchestration means deliberately not integrating immediately. Give one strange piece the whole stage. Let it develop in its own native logic without being prematurely averaged into consensus. Then bring the ensemble back and see whether the whole can now accommodate something it previously couldn’t.

So there are at least three distinct conductor moves:

\boxed{\text{Blend}}
\qquad
\boxed{\text{Solo}}
\qquad
\boxed{\text{Call-and-response}}

And a very capable system knows when each is appropriate.

That turns coordination from making everybody agree into something much richer:

Preserve differentiated voices, control their relationships, and compose their contributions through time.

Harmony with room for solos. The piece needs both.